{"title":"05 Analysis: group differences and MoCA","description":"See group differences, covariate adjustment, and feature-cognition links.","nextTour":"06 Clustering and importance","steps":[{"file":"src/delware_speech/statistical_analysis.py","line":22,"title":"Cohen's d","description":"`compute_cohens_d` — pooled-SD effect size, guarded against tiny groups (n < 2) and zero pooled SD. Used when exactly two diagnostic groups are present."},{"file":"src/delware_speech/statistical_analysis.py","line":34,"title":"The group-comparison engine","description":"`perform_group_comparisons` loops every feature: per-group descriptives, ANOVA + partial eta², Kruskal–Wallis + epsilon², Cohen's d for 2 groups — one output row per feature in `statistical_comparisons.csv`."},{"file":"src/delware_speech/statistical_analysis.py","line":77,"title":"ANOVA with partial eta²","description":"`stats.f_oneway` plus a hand-rolled partial eta-squared (group SS over total SS, lines 79–81) — the effect size the paper reports alongside F."},{"file":"src/delware_speech/statistical_analysis.py","line":87,"title":"Kruskal–Wallis with epsilon²","description":"The non-parametric counterpart (line 87) and its effect size `(H − k + 1)/(n − k)` (line 91). Reported next to the ANOVA row so distribution choice is visible."},{"file":"src/delware_speech/statistical_analysis.py","line":119,"title":"Multiple-testing correction","description":"After the loop, `multipletests` adds FDR (BH) and Bonferroni-adjusted ANOVA p-values — never read the raw `ANOVA_p` column without these when judging significance."},{"file":"src/delware_speech/statistical_analysis.py","line":127,"title":"ANCOVA","description":"`perform_ancova` fits `feature ~ C(Diagnosis) + Age + Education_Years` per feature and reports the group term's coefficient, p, F, and model R² — the covariate-adjusted group effect."},{"file":"src/delware_speech/statistical_analysis.py","line":144,"title":"Minimum usable n","description":"Features with fewer than 10 complete cases are skipped — ANCOVA rows can be fewer than the feature count for sparse features."},{"file":"src/delware_speech/statistical_analysis.py","line":147,"title":"The formula, safely quoted","description":"Feature names become `Q('...')` terms so names with odd characters can't break the patsy formula; the group term is then located by pattern (line 150) because statsmodels expands `C(...)`."},{"file":"src/delware_speech/statistical_analysis.py","line":179,"title":"MoCA correlations","description":"`compute_cognitive_correlations` — per feature: Pearson r (line 203) with R², Spearman rho (line 207), and the regression slope/intercept (line 210) that Figure 6 draws. Minimum 5 complete pairs (line 193)."},{"file":"src/delware_speech/statistical_analysis.py","line":230,"title":"Correlation FDR","description":"The Pearson p column gets the same BH correction — `Pearson_p_FDR` is the column to cite when claiming a feature tracks cognition."}]}